 "result_text": "I changed `Light.set_light` in `src/uiprotect/data/devices.py` so it can control a floodlight that Protect has already switched on or off. I haven't run any tests or tried it against a real floodlight.\n\n**Cause:** `set_light` only sends a change if the `is_led_force_on` setting actually changes. When Protect switches the light on or off itself (motion, schedule), `is_led_force_on` doesn't change. If you then ask for the state `is_led_force_on` already holds, no change is detected and no request is sent. That matches the issue: a light that Protect turned on can't be turned off from Home Assistant.\n\n**Fix:** `set_light` now checks for that mismatch (`is_led_force_on` equals the requested state, but `is_light_on` doesn't). If it finds one, it first sets `is_led_force_on` to the opposite value and saves that. Then it applies the requested state, so the second save is a real change and gets sent. That means two update requests are sent in this case instead of one.\n\nI did this because `isLightOn` is read-only, so it can't be set directly.",
